00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.020
All right, so
let's get stuck in.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:07.110
So from a high level,
what is Cloud Spanner?

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.730
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
So Cloud Spanner,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.420
which we launched this week,
very exciting for all of us

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:16.460
in Spanner and as well as
in general Google Cloud,

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.210
we weren't expected
to have that product,

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.320
is a fully managed, horizontally
scalable, relational database

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.210
service that can have
ACID transaction and SQL

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.210
semantics without
giving up performance

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.020
and high availability.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.460
So that was a lot of words.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.280
Let's unpack.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.580
Basically what Spanner,
or Cloud Spanner does,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.930
is it gives you the
best of both worlds.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.580
So it gives you the best
of traditional, relational

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.910
databases as well as
traditional, non-relational

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.560
or NoSQL databases.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.420
So what that means is we
have schemas, DDL statements,

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:57.220
strong typing, SQL querying,
and much more in that category.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.860
But we also have
horizontal charting

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.880
that can span multiple
availability zones and multiple

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.140
regions across the globe.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.540
And we can do all of
this without compromising

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.060
on performance or
high availability.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.160
And to top it all,
we've offered this

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.130
as a fully managed
service, so you

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.012
don't have to worry
about managing

00:06:17.012 --> 00:06:17.970
this database yourself.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.280
It's completely managed
on the Cloud Platform

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.982
with our SRE team and
the entire infrastructure

00:06:23.982 --> 00:06:24.690
that Google runs.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.148
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, kind of like

00:06:26.148 --> 00:06:28.025
what we did with Bigtable
but for relational.

00:06:28.025 --> 00:06:29.150
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Correct.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:30.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.700
So you mentioned that
you were responsible

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.204
for the internal
launch of Spanner

00:06:35.204 --> 00:06:37.620
and also for Cloud Spanner,
which is the external service.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.590
Could you talk a little
bit about differences

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.230
between those two, a little
bit of the history of how

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.910
this happened?

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:45.930
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Sure.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.140
So let me tackle the
first thing first.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.210
So Cloud Spanner is basically
the internal spanner service

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.060
that we've been running for
multiple years, which has been

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.190
developed for even more years.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:00.540
And it's just a repackaging of
that service as a Google Cloud

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:01.410
Platform service.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.610
So we basically integrated
with the Cloud Platform, things

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.890
such as billing, and
providing the APIs

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:14.362
through the various rest
and gRPC methodologies,

00:07:14.362 --> 00:07:15.070
et cetera, right?

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.530
So it's just the same service.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.900
The underlying infrastructure
is exactly the same.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.940
And it's run by
the same SRE team,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.280
or managed by the same
SRE team that manages

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.611
the internal service as well.

00:07:24.611 --> 00:07:26.610
There is really no
difference except for the way

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:27.717
you access the service.

00:07:27.717 --> 00:07:30.300
MARK MANDEL: So it really sounds
like another example of where

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.909
we've taken stuff that
we've built inside Google,

00:07:32.909 --> 00:07:34.950
and managed inside Google,
and run inside Google,

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.230
and had power, all the stuff
inside Google, and just said,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:38.790
hey, customers, here's
a thing that you

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:39.873
might want to use as well.

00:07:39.873 --> 00:07:41.550
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
You know, so I've

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.290
been asked this
question from customers

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:44.670
as well, because they're
like, are you just throwing

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.086
what you had
internally externally

00:07:46.086 --> 00:07:49.040
and just expecting people to
handle whatever you throw out

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:49.540
there?

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.120
And that's not what we've done.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.430
We've actually
spent a lot of time

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.650
both evaluating
whether Spanner would

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:59.170
be a useful external service
to our Cloud customers,

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:03.750
and then we spent a year or so
in our Early Access Program,

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.020
where we had customers sort of
working with us as partners,

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.680
evaluating sort of what
the API should look like,

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.380
how we should offer it,
what are the features that

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.540
need to be there.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:18.300
So what do our enterprise
customers actually need,

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.090
because Spanner itself
has a huge surface area

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:22.590
of features in API.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.450
We didn't make it
complicated and confusing

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.200
for the customers.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.740
And there was actually a demand
for something like Spanner.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:33.330
And then we developed that
as the Cloud Spanner service

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.200
and launched it.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:39.929
So going back to the
question about the history.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:43.080
So when I joined Google,
I was a software engineer.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.930
And I was working
in AdWords' backend,

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.230
and everything was
MySQL everywhere.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.530
And it was fun.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:56.880
And then everyone started
migrating to Spanner.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.210
And for me, it was pretty
crazy because at the same time

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.650
that we were
migrating to Spanner,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:05.020
everybody else was migrating
to non-relational databases.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.920
Why did we create, rather
than moving to Spanner,

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:09.810
or the question would
be, why would you

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.940
use Spanner instead of
like Bigtable or something

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:12.441
like that?

00:09:12.441 --> 00:09:14.440
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: That's
an easy answer, right?

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.120
Bigtable is not a
relational database.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:19.710
It's a wide column store, or
key value store at the end,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:20.650
at the bottom of it.

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.410
It's a NoSQL system.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:26.310
And spanner is a, essentially,
traditional relational system

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.060
with scale.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.610
But to go back to the history--

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:35.700
so basically about
2007-ish or so, Google

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.150
was facing the issues
that current enterprise,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.860
or just current
companies face today,

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:45.210
which is they really need
relational transactional

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.640
semantics, because that makes
development of applications

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.210
easier and it makes reasoning
about things that you really

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.060
care about, like your bank
accounts and things like that,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.490
much easier because
you have transactions.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:10:00.947
But with the explosion of
data, there is so much data,

00:10:00.947 --> 00:10:03.030
and you want so many more
insights from that data,

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.530
that a traditional
single box sort

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:11.000
of vertical solution for the
database doesn't actually work.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:14.290
So you need to go to NoSQL or
something that's scaled out.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.570
And that becomes
really problematic

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:19.150
because if you choose
the NoSQL solution,

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.900
then you have to build all
the transactional semantics

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.460
and such in the application.

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:26.290
You don't get SQL.

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:27.760
You get something
SQL-like, which

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.480
you have to train
your developers when

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.355
using this exact esoteric
thing, et cetera,

00:10:31.355 --> 00:10:32.230
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:34.120
So we were facing
the same challenge.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.160
And so a group of
engineers, like Jeff Dean,

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.440
and Sanjay, and Andrew
Fikes, and a whole bunch

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.060
of other people
kind of got together

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.950
and started to look at
what can we do about this.

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.130
And that's where kind of
Spanner came from, right?

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:52.980
So we wanted to have
a transactional system

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:57.790
with relational semantics,
and strong schemas and types,

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.490
which also provided
horizontal scalability,

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.740
with global presence,
and those qualities,

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:07.660
because that's what Google
applications themselves need,

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:08.360
like AdWords.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.151
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And
then many more since then.

00:11:11.151 --> 00:11:13.060
And so that's kind of
the history of Spanner.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.060
And as we developed it, we
realized that actually SQL

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:20.860
is really useful as a way
to sort of access this data.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.420
And it's very prevalent.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:25.930
And it makes the lives of
the developers much easier

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:28.150
to use, like, standard SQL.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:33.040
And so we built in sort of
a SQL engine into Spanner,

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.640
which is what we
offer now today,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.750
which uses what we
call standard SQL,

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.480
which is ANSI C 2011
compliant, with extensions.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So does that
include things like joins,

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.670
or stuff like that,
like fancy Google things

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.472
that you can do with SQL?

00:11:47.472 --> 00:11:48.430
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yes.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:52.220
It's a query-only, so
select-only SQL interface.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:53.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.060
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
But yes, it has joins.

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:57.490
You can join across
many tables, any number

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.020
of tables in your database.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:00.520
You can do inner
joins, outer joins.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:01.602
And you can do indexes.

00:12:01.602 --> 00:12:03.310
We have strong secondary
indexes like you

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.310
have with any other database.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:08.080
Those indexes are
called local or global,

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.600
depending on whether you use
interleaving or not, which,

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:14.180
again, you can look these
things up on the site.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I watched a talk

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.810
not that long ago from someone
that used to work at Google.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.970
I don't know, maybe you
know her because she

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.580
used to work in that
team, Neha Narula?

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.420
I don't know if you've
ever heard about her,

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:27.850
but she gave a really good talk.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.075
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: I have not.

00:12:28.075 --> 00:12:28.300
OK.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.133
FRANCESC CAMPOY: She
gave a really good talk

00:12:30.133 --> 00:12:34.510
about serial visibility, and
like the CAP theorem and stuff

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:35.140
like that.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:38.070
And she talks a lot about,
what is consistency,

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.670
and all the
different definitions

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:43.479
of consistency, which is
like, what is consistency?

00:12:43.479 --> 00:12:45.520
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Do you
mean global consistency

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:46.660
or consistency in general?

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:49.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What is the
consistency we care about?

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:50.983
Because I've heard about
global consistency,

00:12:50.983 --> 00:12:54.550
sequential consistency,
and causal consistency.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:55.420
There's many kinds.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:57.000
So could you tell
us a little bit

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.810
about what is this
thing that people

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.730
talk about when they
say consistency related

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.062
to Spanner and Cloud Spanner?

00:13:02.062 --> 00:13:04.020
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: So
first of all, consistency

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:05.890
is a whole topic
in and of itself.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:07.717
And people get very
religious about it.

00:13:07.717 --> 00:13:08.800
I don't want to get there.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, good.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:12.890
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: But to
put it simply, basically

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:16.210
there are eventual consistent
systems, or eventually

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:18.130
consistent systems,
which basically

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:21.040
means you do an operation
and eventually, we

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.020
can't guarantee
when, but eventually

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.720
all parts of the system
will see that change.

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, Bigtable
would be one of those?

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:29.850
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
One of those, exactly.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:30.430
MARK MANDEL: Data store.

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:32.554
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Data
store is also one of those.

00:13:32.554 --> 00:13:34.870
And then there is a sort
of causal consistencies.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:38.210
If you are in the path of
something happening, right?

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.900
So if you observe one
thing and then observe

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.670
another thing as a causal
relationship between those,

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:46.240
if there is a causal
relationship between those two,

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:47.560
then you will see the outcome,

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.250
If there isn't, then you
don't see a change reflected.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:54.940
And then what Spanner has,
or what Cloud Spanner offers,

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.987
is strong consistency,
which again,

00:13:57.987 --> 00:14:00.320
depending on who you talk to,
has different definitions.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.020
But basically strong
consistency means

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:08.680
that if you commit a change,
and if the system says

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:10.970
it's committed, as in
the system accepts it,

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:15.310
then all parts of the system
see that change instantaneously

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:16.390
or immediately.

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:20.094
There is no concept of I saw
some change but you didn't--

00:14:20.094 --> 00:14:21.760
if you queried the
system from somewhere

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.700
else, or a different point
in time, or whatever.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:27.880
There is no such thing
as eventually getting

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:28.840
the information.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.670
You either have the
information or you don't.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.597
And you have it together
or you don't have it.

00:14:33.597 --> 00:14:35.180
You have it all
together or you don't.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:38.380
And this is kind of what
traditional databases offer

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:39.580
as strong consistency.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:43.360
That's what ACID transactions
actually mean, as you know,

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.180
Atomicity, Consistency,
Isolation, and Durability.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.610
And there is this consistency.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:49.026
MARK MANDEL: So I
want to bring up

00:14:49.026 --> 00:14:50.490
the magic elephant in the room.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:51.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The magic elephant.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.633
MARK MANDEL: The magic elephant.

00:14:52.633 --> 00:14:56.110
So we've talked a little bit
about CAP theorem, right?

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.780
So consistency, availability
partition tolerance.

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.920
Does that mean,
like, we beat it?

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:01.420
Or like--

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:02.650
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: You
mean beat the CAP theorem?

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:03.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, did
we beat it, as like,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:05.260
we defied laws of physics?

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.650
Like what are we doing here?

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:07.450
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: So
we certainly did not

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:09.100
defy the laws of physics.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.866
Let's put that out there.

00:15:11.866 --> 00:15:13.990
So the question is, does
Spanner break CAP theorem?

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:15.280
Not really.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.020
What Spanner does
is it always chooses

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.090
consistency over availability.

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:25.240
So it always chooses C over
A in face of partitions.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.790
So CAP theorem is consistency,
availability, and partitions,

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:30.240
and how do you-- or network
partitions to be specific--

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.000
and how does a
system handle that.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.120
Do you choose C or A in
the face of partitions

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.680
is the very basic answer.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:42.280
And what we say is that Spanner
always opts for consistency,

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:44.140
hence we are a strongly
consistent system.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:50.020
However, because we can leverage
Google's networking and network

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.450
stack, which are
highly optimized,

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:57.430
and because we've built Spanner
to be a highly optimal system

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.160
when it comes to
these things, we

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.680
can actually get very high
availability and performance

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:03.740
as well.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:07.990
So Spanner offers up to
five nines of availability.

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:11.980
And we can be this strongly,
or highly available,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:15.580
even in the face of partitions
because of the optimized Google

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:17.990
network and our own
optimized stack.

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.830
So I would recommend that
anybody who's really interested

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:24.920
go look at Eric Brewer's
paper on this exact topic.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.770
It's called "Spanner
and the CAP theorem."

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:28.270
We linked to it
from our website,

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.940
which is
cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:33.724
It's a full research paper
written by the person who

00:16:33.724 --> 00:16:34.640
wrote the CAP theorem.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.147
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:16:36.147 --> 00:16:37.480
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: He has more--

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:38.410
that paper has more.

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:40.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think he
knows what the CAP theorem is.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:41.290
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.015
He can get into that
in much more detail.

00:16:43.015 --> 00:16:43.890
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:46.180
All right, well, so you
mentioned a little bit before,

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.130
basically where you
said there's a SQL

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:49.660
query, a language
that you could use

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:51.350
for querying, so
basically listing data

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:53.540
and aggregating and
stepping through it.

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.560
So if I'm a developer, how
do I go in and alter it,

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:57.669
or add data to it?

00:16:57.669 --> 00:16:59.210
What does that
interaction look like?

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.040
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: So first of
all, we have DDL statements.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:02.581
So if you want to
change your schema,

00:17:02.581 --> 00:17:04.810
you use general DDL
statements, like Alter Table,

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.490
Drop Table, Add
Column, whatever.

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:08.980
The interesting
thing about Spanner

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:11.170
is that all the
schema changes happen

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:13.270
online, which means you
don't have to bring down

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:17.560
your database in order for the
database to reconfigure itself,

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:21.099
where we lay out the data
for these things, which

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:22.000
is super cool.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.180
Also if you want to alter
data, Read, Write, Edit,

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.420
do CRUD operations,
then we actually

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:31.480
have to go through the
programmatic API to do that.

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:35.981
We don't offer Insert, Update,
Delete in SQL currently.

00:17:35.981 --> 00:17:37.480
We have plans to
provide that later.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.000
But currently we
don't have that.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.880
So you have to use
a programmatic API.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.500
However, we've tried to
make that API as close

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.370
to SQL as possible.

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:48.630
So you do do inserts, like the
Key Words, or Insert, Update,

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:49.266
or Delete.

00:17:49.266 --> 00:17:49.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, cool.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.910
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And
we have a mutation API

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:54.340
so you can put all these
different statements together

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:55.460
into mutations.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.860
For reads, you can
use the read API.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.730
If you're familiar with read
APIs from NoSQL systems,

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.380
it's a much easier transition.

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:04.150
And you can also use SQL.

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:07.160
So you can do Select,
Star from Table,

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:10.260
where x equal to
foo or something.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
the API you mentioned,

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:15.840
not the SQL API but the other
one, is that based on gRPC?

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:16.840
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:20.319
So we offer the client
libraries in what

00:18:20.319 --> 00:18:22.360
we call all silver languages,
but right now we're

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:24.654
offering them in Java,
Go, Python, and Node.js.

00:18:24.654 --> 00:18:25.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:28.210
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And
we'll have PHP, and Ruby,

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:29.552
and C-sharp later in the year.

00:18:29.552 --> 00:18:30.760
MARK MANDEL: Very, very cool.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.830
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And we
expect that most customers--

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.205
because these are the most
popular languages that we know

00:18:35.205 --> 00:18:37.542
of and we've seen requests for--

00:18:37.542 --> 00:18:39.250
most customers should
be able to just use

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:41.740
these client libraries
instead of having

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:45.730
to code against the REST
API or the gRPC API.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.980
We also have a
JDBC driver that's

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:50.290
a read-only JDBC
driver, for those

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:52.360
who are interested
in that or are more

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:53.610
familiar with those semantics.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.068
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:18:55.068 --> 00:18:56.510
MARK MANDEL: That is very cool.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:57.426
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:57.426 --> 00:19:01.450
So let's talk about if
I'm a developer-- well,

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.530
I am a developer.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:03.340
MARK MANDEL: You
are a developer.

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:05.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If I was
to create a new project,

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:06.580
why would I use Spanner?

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:09.170
Like in what use cases
are you saying, like,

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.080
oh, yeah you should go
with Spanner, rather

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:13.660
than, I don't know,
like Cloud SQL, or maybe

00:19:13.660 --> 00:19:14.881
Datastore or Bigtable?

00:19:14.881 --> 00:19:15.880
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Sure.

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:17.546
That's a very common
question, actually.

00:19:17.546 --> 00:19:20.590
And I think once you
look at what you're

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:24.220
trying to accomplish with
your data storage system,

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:26.100
then it becomes much easier.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:29.650
So as I said, Spanner
is scale out relational.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.960
So if you have a
relational data,

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:35.500
and you really like MySQL,
or all your data is in MySQL,

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:38.230
and it fits in your
MySQL instance,

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:41.350
or Cloud SQL instance,
then you should just

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:43.990
use Cloud SQL to
manage MySQL Service.

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:49.840
However, if you're running out
of the capacity for that MySQL

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:53.350
or Cloud SQL instance, because
you have way too much data

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:56.620
or way too much workload
that cannot be handled by one

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:01.060
vertical solution, then
Spanner is a great alternative.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:06.610
Because if you're running into
the seams of your MySQL box,

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:10.840
then you have two alternatives--
either to go to a sharded MySQL

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:16.030
solution, which has a whole set
of issues that you have to deal

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.100
with managing and
maintaining that,

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:21.400
or you then have to
transition to NoSQL,

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:23.327
which you give up
all those cool--

00:20:23.327 --> 00:20:24.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Goodbye transactions.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:26.109
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Correct.

00:20:26.109 --> 00:20:28.400
So in that case, you don't
have to make that trade-off.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:31.300
You go to Spanner, basically.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.340
In terms of when
to use Datastore,

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.200
or Bigtable, and Spanner--

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:38.540
as I said, Bigtable
is a key value store.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.970
It's a wide column store.

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:44.920
It doesn't have SQL, or
strongly typed schemas,

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:50.980
or multi-row transactions,
and things like that.

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:54.190
But it's great for sort of
large-scale data analytics

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:56.440
and things like that.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:57.610
So you can totally see that.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:03.160
Also it's not
synchronously replicated.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.070
So Spanner, by default, out
of the box is synchronously

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:09.670
replicated, which means
when you write to Spanner,

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:12.190
you're actually writing to
n different replicas, which

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:13.670
are, in our regional
configuration,

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.380
has three replicas, whereas--

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.290
and they're not
eventually consistent.

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:18.340
They're strong.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:22.600
It's synchronously replicated
without compromising

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:23.460
our performance.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:24.509
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:21:24.509 --> 00:21:26.050
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
And then Datastore

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:30.190
is an object store, essentially,
or a document store.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:33.400
So again, it doesn't have
the same relational semantics

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:36.190
that Spanner or MySQL have.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:38.620
So again, if you don't
need relational semantics,

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:39.772
or if you just want to--

00:21:39.772 --> 00:21:41.730
it's a great product to
get started on as well.

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:45.190
Like if you have low QPS
or low-query numbers,

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:49.240
and you just want to get started
and use a consistent data

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:53.500
store, which is, again,
multi-region and available,

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:56.030
highly available, then I think
Datastore is a great option.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:57.940
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm really curious--

00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:59.232
BigQuery.

00:21:59.232 --> 00:22:00.940
I know a lot of people
who will take data

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:02.999
from maybe Datastore
or other places,

00:22:02.999 --> 00:22:04.540
push it into BigQuery
so that they're

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:07.720
able to do large-scale
querying for analytics.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:12.260
Sounds like Cloud Spanner will
handle that workload as well?

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:13.950
Where does the line
get drawn there?

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:16.366
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: So I think
this is the OLTP versus OLAP

00:22:16.366 --> 00:22:17.470
conversation.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:20.110
Because BigQuery is
ultimately a data

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:24.070
warehousing solution but in
the Cloud and with scalability.

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:29.781
So it's really meant
for running OLAP style.

00:22:29.781 --> 00:22:31.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What is OLAP?

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:32.930
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Sorry.

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:37.187
Yeah, so OLTP stands for
Online Transaction Processing.

00:22:37.187 --> 00:22:38.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.680
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And OLAP
stands for Online Analytics

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:42.370
Processing.

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:42.735
MARK MANDEL: Right.

00:22:42.735 --> 00:22:43.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I see, OK.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:45.970
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: So you know,
the cool thing about BigQuery

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.610
is that you can actually
import data from different data

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.640
sources, and then combine them,
and then run analytics on them,

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:54.674
so do aggregations and
roll-ups and things.

00:22:54.674 --> 00:22:56.590
You can sort of have as
much data as you need,

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:57.860
or as little data as you need.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:00.650
And you can run a bunch
of queries on this.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:03.220
Spanner is your
operational database.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:05.350
BigQuery is not an
operational database.

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:08.079
So Spanner is where you
put your production data.

00:23:08.079 --> 00:23:10.370
That's where you run your
mission critical application.

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:14.020
And then if you want to combine
that data with other sources,

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:17.197
or if you want to run a
billing pipeline that's

00:23:17.197 --> 00:23:19.030
monthly aggregations
or something like that,

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.940
then you transfer that
data into BigQuery.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.370
Then you can join it
with other data sources

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:26.817
or just run long running
operations on it as well.

00:23:26.817 --> 00:23:28.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
