and whether it's really
priced for smaller companies.

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:01.510
And I would say we put a lot
of thought in our pricing.

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:04.510
And I think we are very
competitively priced

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:07.240
for the value that
Cloud Spanner offers.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:12.070
If you really look across
the Cloud data storage

00:31:12.070 --> 00:31:14.380
offerings with
competitors and stuff,

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:16.800
I think we are
pretty well-priced.

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.290
You know, you're getting a fully
managed, very high availability

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:20.891
service.

00:31:20.891 --> 00:31:22.390
So imagine you not
having to get out

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:23.765
of bed in the
middle of the night

00:31:23.765 --> 00:31:25.390
because your database
machine crashed,

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:27.970
or because it's running
out of memory so you

00:31:27.970 --> 00:31:29.620
have to provision more memory.

00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:33.050
Just to talk about pricing a
little bit really quickly--

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:35.890
so when you buy a
node, you actually

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:37.447
get three replicas of the node.

00:31:37.447 --> 00:31:38.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:38.680
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.790
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: We have three
different availability zones.

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:41.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
was going to ask you

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:44.180
that because that is not the
case for Bigtable for instance.

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.065
In Bigtable you need to create
a cluster with three nodes.

00:31:47.065 --> 00:31:48.760
Here one node is enough.

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:50.343
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
One node is enough.

00:31:50.343 --> 00:31:51.760
It's the lowest.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:54.940
We do recommend that
you create or get

00:31:54.940 --> 00:32:00.250
three nodes just for production
grade availability and traffic.

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:01.660
But really you can buy one node.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:03.326
But the point is that
one node gives you

00:32:03.326 --> 00:32:05.380
three replicas, like
three nodes and three

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:09.220
different availability zones,
all of which are live nodes.

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:10.714
They can all serve data.

00:32:10.714 --> 00:32:12.130
And they're not
just sitting there

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:13.870
as a master slave
kind of configuration,

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:15.340
because as I said
earlier, Spanner

00:32:15.340 --> 00:32:17.290
is synchronously replicated.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.320
So there is no-- it's all
sort of what would be called

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:22.210
master-master replication.

00:32:22.210 --> 00:32:25.820
You get fully managed,
high availability.

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:26.950
You get SQL.

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:30.820
You get the scalability.

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:33.550
And you get these regional and
multi-region configurations

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:34.470
when they launch.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:36.850
So there's a lot
there that you're

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:38.980
getting out of
Spanner for the price

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:40.105
that we're offering it for.

00:32:40.105 --> 00:32:42.188
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, I
think it's definitely worth

00:32:42.188 --> 00:32:44.200
taking into account
the fact that you don't

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:46.720
need to take care of
like, what happens

00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:48.610
if your database disappears?

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:50.860
Because I don't know,
we've heard about stories

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:52.270
of these things happen.

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.540
And the fact that you don't
even need to care about it

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:59.060
at all, that is, I don't know,
if I was running a company,

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.212
I'd definitely consider it.

00:33:00.212 --> 00:33:00.920
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:01.920
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:04.720
It's a trade off between
how much time do you

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:06.280
and your developers
and your IT staff

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:10.360
spend managing your database
versus building applications

00:33:10.360 --> 00:33:13.000
and growing your business, and
what the trade off looks like.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:14.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
the way to put it.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:15.290
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

