00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:06.534
sent to us by the
amazing Jessie Frazelle.

00:35:06.534 --> 00:35:07.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:11.830
So as part of her,
basically, let's

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.050
call it integration
into Google, she

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:15.970
decided to basically
move all her stuff

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:17.290
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.330
And she came up with
this little problem.

00:35:19.330 --> 00:35:23.260
She wanted to run an SMTP
server, so a mail server,

00:35:23.260 --> 00:35:24.800
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:26.690
And turns out that
it doesn't work.

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:30.010
So she asked me, how
is this and why not?

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:33.760
And it is in the
Docs, but I think

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:36.130
that it is something that
could be a little bit

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:38.800
surprising, so I guess
it's worth discussing.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:39.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:41.610
So it is definitely
worth noting,

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:43.360
Google Compute
Engine specifically

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:45.160
doesn't allow
outbound connections

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:52.060
on 25, 465, and 487, basically
because Spam, and abuse,

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:52.914
and bad things.

00:35:52.914 --> 00:35:53.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:57.100
Basically, there's this
thing called IP reputation.

00:35:57.100 --> 00:36:00.220
So if you're sending
emails, the IP where

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:02.380
the email is coming
from, IP forward,

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:04.260
is actually very important.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:09.100
So if we allowed everyone
to send emails from there,

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:11.690
the IPs would probably lose
their reputation quite fast.

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:12.440
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:13.856
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So it's something

00:36:13.856 --> 00:36:15.609
we don't really want
to do because it

00:36:15.609 --> 00:36:16.900
would be bad for our customers.

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:17.608
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:36:17.608 --> 00:36:20.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And also because it

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:22.490
would be a very easy way
to generate lots of Spam,

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:24.250
and Spam is not good.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:27.310
So then we have
these limitations.

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:29.780
But we can work around
it quite easily.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:34.030
You can use some services
that are already available.

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:35.990
And we have documentation
of how to use them.

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:39.190
So you can use Sendgrid,
Mailgun, or Mailjet.

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:41.140
Those are the ones we
have for recommendation.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:42.820
And I think you use one of them.

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:43.570
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.190
So they all come
with free tiers.

00:36:45.190 --> 00:36:47.000
I use Sendgrid, personally.

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:48.850
So Sendgrid comes
with a free trial

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:52.570
of up to 12,000 transactional
emails free each month.

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:55.450
My blog does not send out
anywhere near that many emails,

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:56.929
so it works really, really well.

00:36:56.929 --> 00:36:59.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Even though
you send it to all of your fans?

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:01.330
MARK MANDEL: All of
my fans, like my mom,

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:03.030
and a couple of cousins.

00:37:03.030 --> 00:37:05.704
FRANCESC CAMPOY: He has
a big family, 12,000.

00:37:05.704 --> 00:37:06.389
Cool.

00:37:06.389 --> 00:37:07.930
And then there's
also another option,

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:12.100
which is you can send
email through Google.

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:14.530
We have an SMTP relay service.

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:18.700
And basically what you do is
you send it through Google Apps,

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:20.710
even though the
email is not Gmail.

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:22.210
So there's these
little servers that

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.480
allows you to send it from
your server but through Google.

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:28.900
And all documentation will be
linked from the show notes.

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:32.047
So if this is something
that you want to do,

00:37:32.047 --> 00:37:33.130
go read the documentation.

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:37.870
And of course, remember
that even though you

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:39.490
might use these
services, you still

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:42.250
need to add firewalls to make
sure that the traffic actually

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:45.130
goes through, as we
discussed in the networking

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:47.015
episode we had a long time ago.

00:37:47.015 --> 00:37:47.890
You can go find them.

00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:49.450
And they're very interesting.

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:50.200
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

